# Packaging the website

The website is a small web application: a page the browser loads, a client that boots it, and a server that renders it first for a reader and a crawler. Vite bundles the client; the server runs under Bun. This chapter is the files around the code — the page, the manifest, the TypeScript settings, and the build.

## The page {Tangle}[index.html]

The page is the shell the application fills. It carries an empty `#root` for the view to mount into, and the module that boots the client.

The site is set in two families. Space Grotesk carries the headings, and Lilex carries everything else — the running text as well as the code, since the whole site is set in mono. Lilex has no Google entry, so it arrives as three `@font-face` rules pointing at its own files, with IBM Plex Mono standing behind it in the stack for the moment before they land. Noto Color Emoji comes along for the emoji the documentation shows.

The server rewrites three spots: the `#root` it fills with the rendered body, the head where it inlines the model, and the `<title>` it replaces with the route's own. The first two it finds by exact text, so the strings `<div id="root"></div>` and `</head>` matter as much as the markup; the title it finds by its element, so the words below are a default no reader sees.

=>

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>loom — programs written to be read</title>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Space+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500;600&family=Noto+Color+Emoji&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
    <style>
      @font-face {
        font-family: 'Lilex';
        src: url('https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@fontsource/lilex/files/lilex-latin-400-normal.woff2') format('woff2');
        font-weight: 400;
        font-display: swap;
      }
      @font-face {
        font-family: 'Lilex';
        src: url('https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@fontsource/lilex/files/lilex-latin-500-normal.woff2') format('woff2');
        font-weight: 500;
        font-display: swap;
      }
      @font-face {
        font-family: 'Lilex';
        src: url('https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@fontsource/lilex/files/lilex-latin-600-normal.woff2') format('woff2');
        font-weight: 600;
        font-display: swap;
      }
    </style>
    <script type="module" src="/src/client.ts"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="root"></div>
  </body>
</html>

## The manifest {Tangle}[package.json]

The manifest names the package, its commands, and its dependencies. The site holds its own components, so it leans on only a few packages: `@athrio/foldkit-ssr` and `@athrio/foldkit-hydration` for the render and the adopt, `foldkit` and `effect` beneath them, and `marked` to turn the documentation's woven prose into HTML. The type comes from the page's own head rather than from a package.

Before the site runs or builds, one step prepares the content it reads. It weaves the documentation, which is its own corpus of prose sitting beside this one, into the JSON the docs page renders. That JSON derives from a loom, so nothing the documentation shows is a copy of anything — change a chapter and the page changes with it. The landing's example needs no such step: the document it types is data in the section itself, since it is a teaching document rather than a corpus of Loom's own.

Each weave is pointed at a *file* rather than at a directory, because weaving takes the corpus around the path it is given and a directory resolves to the one above it.

The `@athrio/loom-devtools` package is a development dependency alone, since its plugin runs only while Vite serves and never reaches the bundle.

Development runs the same server production runs, so a developer reads the pages a reader would. The `dev` command starts two processes for that: Vite, which holds the client and answers on its own port, and the server in front of it on 5199, told by `LOOM_DEV` to render each page as it is asked for. Only the server is watched. It restarts on a change and Vite carries on, which is why a change shows up on the next reload rather than in the running page.

=>

{
  "name": "loom-website",
  "type": "module",
  "private": true,
  "version": "0.0.0",
  "scripts": {
    "weave:docs": "bunx @athrio/loom weave ../docs/index.loom src/docs.woven.json",
    "content": "bun run weave:docs",
    "predev": "bun run content",
    "dev": "bunx --bun vite & vite=$!; trap 'kill $vite' EXIT; LOOM_DEV=1 bun --watch src/server.ts",
    "prebuild": "bun run content",
    "build": "bunx --bun vite build",
    "serve": "bun src/server.ts",
    "preview": "bunx --bun vite preview --port 5199"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@athrio/foldkit-ssr": "workspace:*",
    "@athrio/foldkit-hydration": "workspace:*",
    "foldkit": "workspace:*",
    "effect": "4.0.0-beta.97",
    "marked": "^18.0.5"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@athrio/loom-devtools": "workspace:*",
    "vite": "^8.1.3"
  }
}

## The browser TypeScript config {Tangle}[tsconfig.json]

The client runs in the browser and the server on Bun, and their type environments cannot share one program. Foldkit's runtime reads Vite's `import.meta.hot`, and Bun's platform types declare a different `import.meta.hot`; in one program the two collide, and the client's reads stop type-checking. So the configuration splits. This one checks the browser half — the client, the view, and everything they reach — reading Vite's types through the ambient declaration beside it, and leaves the two server-only files to the server config.

=>

{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "target": "es2022",
    "lib": ["es2023", "dom", "dom.iterable"],
    "types": ["bun"],
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["src"],
  "exclude": ["src/server.ts", "src/browser-globals.ts"]
}

## The server TypeScript config {Tangle}[tsconfig.server.json]

This checks the server half — the server and its browser shim — under Bun. Because the server never imports the client runtime, Vite's `import.meta.hot` never enters its program, and the collision the browser config avoids by exclusion this one avoids by construction.

=>

{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "target": "es2022",
    "lib": ["es2023", "dom", "dom.iterable"],
    "types": ["bun"],
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["src/server.ts", "src/browser-globals.ts", "vite.config.ts"]
}

## The Vite types {Tangle}[src/vite-env.d.ts]

The client reaches for Vite's ambient types — `import.meta.hot` above all, which Foldkit's runtime reads — so this one line brings them in.

=>

/// <reference types="vite/client" />

## The build {Tangle}[vite.config.ts]

The build is Vite with the page as its input, targeting a runtime recent enough that the Effect and Foldkit output needs no down-levelling. It writes the page and its assets to `dist`, where the server reads them.

It also adds the Loom Devtools plugin, `loomDevtools`. The plugin runs the Devtools while Vite serves — the notes server over a SQLite file, and the review bar injected into every page — so the website carries the devtools in development the way a browser's own ride a page, with nothing else to start. It runs only while Vite serves, never in the production build, so nothing of it reaches what the server renders. The project it is named for is the one its notes belong to.

While serving, Vite sits behind the site's own server rather than in front of a reader, so it takes the port the server proxies to — `VITE_PORT`, or 5200, the same default the server reads. Its hot client rides along with every page and opens a socket back to wherever that page came from. Pages now come from the site's own server, which speaks no such protocol, so `clientPort` aims the socket at Vite by hand.

What arrives down that socket is a reload rather than a patch. Foldkit accepts no update in place, so Vite falls back to reloading the page, and a reload is a whole render through the server — which is the point of running the server here at all. Foldkit hears the reload coming and preserves the model across it, so the page returns where the developer left it.

Naming the site a multi-page one drops Vite's single-page fallback. A path outside the site's routes then comes back not-found in development exactly as it does in production, rather than quietly returning the shell. The server asks for `index.html` by name, which a multi-page Vite still transforms.

=>

import { defineConfig } from 'vite'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'
import { loomDevtools } from '@athrio/loom-devtools/vite'

const vitePort = Number(process.env.VITE_PORT ?? 5200)

export default defineConfig({
  esbuild: { target: 'es2022' },
  appType: 'mpa',
  plugins: [loomDevtools({ project: 'loom-website' })],
  server: {
    port: vitePort,
    hmr: { clientPort: vitePort },
  },
  build: {
    target: 'es2022',
    outDir: 'dist',
    rollupOptions: {
      input: { index: fileURLToPath(new URL('index.html', import.meta.url)) },
    },
  },
})
